From: Oliver Sargison <sarol263@student.otago.ac.nz>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 19:32:03 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 39/76] Plutonium: per-thread permission grants
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

EXO-TAB-PERMISSIONS (docs/features/TAB_FEATURES.md, "Grants and threads"):
a permission can be allowed or blocked for a thread (the tab's origin tag)
instead of once or everywhere.

- The threads' decisions, (thread, origin, permission) → allow or block,
  are in local state ("plutonium.thread_permissions"). Chrome stores
  nothing for them.
- PermissionContextBase::SetThreadDecisionCallback(): where Chrome's own
  setting asks, GetPermissionStatus() reports the thread's decision for a
  frame of one of its tabs, and a request answered for the thread (which
  the prompt dismisses) resolves with it. navigator.permissions.query()
  says so too.
- The prompt offers "thread" first (except for automatic downloads). The
  answer stores the decision, dismisses the request, and resets the
  embargo counts, so it doesn't count as a dismissal.
- Tab commands: "permission-request" {permissions, origin?, by?} asks the
  user (marked as the agent's with by: "agent"; agents still can't allow)
  and resolves with the answer; "permissions" lists the tab's thread's
  decisions and Chrome's own for its origin; "permission-revoke" takes one
  back.
- WindowBroker.RunPermissionCommand / permissionCommand(): "list" and
  "revoke" any thread's decisions; a shell revokes a thread's when it
  archives or deletes it.
